---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WeeklyAnimation from '../components/WeeklyAnimation.astro';
import LatestContent from '../components/LatestContent.astro';
import SpeakerCTA from '../components/SpeakerCTA.astro';
import WhatsNew from '../components/WhatsNew.astro';
import { currentTheme } from '../themes/theme-config';

const theme = currentTheme;

// Boarding-pass stats. All real, set as flight data.
const boardingData = [
  { k: 'Countries', v: '80+' },
  { k: 'Spoken in', v: '50+' },
  { k: 'Continents', v: '7' },
  { k: 'Books', v: '15' },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE SAFETY CARD. Six numbered panels. Each one keeps the airline instruction
// exactly as it is written on a real card, and then says what that instruction
// means when the aircraft is an AI deployment inside a finance organization.
// The instructions are not decoration. Every one of these is a failure Edward
// has watched happen, and several of them happened to him.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const panels = [
  {
    no: '01',
    icon: 'brace',
    signage: 'Adopt the brace position when the model sounds certain',
    original: 'Brace, brace. Head down. Stay down.',
    body: `A model that's wrong sounds exactly like a model that's right: same speed, same tidy formatting, and I have never once seen a wrong answer turn up looking nervous about it. Everybody builds a plan for the system that says "I don't know," and then what actually arrives is a confident wrong number at 6PM on the last day of a close (ask me how I know, but buy me a coffee first). So the brace position is simple: anything going to an auditor or a board or frankly anybody who can fine you gets checked against the source by a human before it leaves the building. Confidence is the factory setting on these things and it is not a signal, and I have watched extremely smart people read it as one anyway.`,
  },
  {
    no: '02',
    icon: 'mask',
    signage: 'Secure your own data before assisting others',
    original: 'Fit your own mask before helping other passengers.',
    body: `Every company I talk to wants to automate somebody else's job first. Sales would like the forecast automated. Finance would like the pipeline automated. Meanwhile the general ledger has 4 versions of the same cost center and 2 people who know which one is real, and neither of them is in the meeting. Fix your own chart of accounts before you offer to fix anyone's process. (This is the panel I get argued with about most, almost always by someone whose data is worse than they think it is.)`,
  },
  {
    no: '03',
    icon: 'exit',
    signage: 'Your nearest exit may be behind you',
    original: 'Take a moment to locate your nearest exit, bearing in mind that it may be behind you.',
    body: `I've read a lot of AI deployment plans. I can count on one hand the ones that said what happens if you turn it off. Not if it fails, but if it works and 18 months later you want out anyway. Who still knows how to close the books by hand? How long would that take? If the honest answers are nobody and forever, what you've got is a support contract you can't get out of, and you signed it yourself. Write the rollback before departure (a page is plenty, nobody is asking you for a runbook), and put somebody in the exit row who is actually willing to open the door.`,
  },
  {
    no: '04',
    icon: 'cushion',
    signage: 'Your vendor agreement is not a flotation device',
    original: 'Your seat cushion may be used as a flotation device.',
    body: `Read what your contract actually promises. Most AI service agreements cover uptime. Uptime is the vendor's problem, not yours, and very few of these agreements say anything at all about the output being wrong. So ask the vendor's counsel, in writing (in writing, I mean it, and keep the reply), who carries the liability when the model produces a number that ends up in a filing. It takes about 10 minutes to get an answer and the answer is you. I have now watched 3 different finance teams find this out at the worst available moment.`,
  },
  {
    no: '05',
    icon: 'stow',
    signage: 'Some phases of flight still require a human',
    original: 'Electronic devices must be switched off and stowed during takeoff and landing.',
    body: `The rule was never really about phones being dangerous. Takeoff and landing are when things go wrong, so that's when everyone's attention belongs on the actual aircraft. Same goes for a close, an audit, a first run of any process, and anything a regulator will eventually read. Cruise is where agents earn their keep: reconciliations, variance narratives, first drafts of basically anything, and about 30 other things nobody wants to be doing at 11PM (they are very good first drafts, and I say that as somebody who has been handed some deeply bad ones by humans). The 5 minutes at each end stay human. Cruise was always where the money was, so holding the ends back costs you almost nothing.`,
  },
  {
    no: '06',
    icon: 'crosscheck',
    signage: 'Doors to manual. Cross check.',
    original: 'Cabin crew, doors to manual and cross check.',
    body: `Aviation's best idea is that a second person confirms out loud that the first person did the thing. It costs about 4 seconds and it isn't a technology, so nobody has ever tried to sell it to you. Every finance team already knows this one as segregation of duties, and most of them drop it the moment the work is done by software instead of by a person and nobody says so out loud, because software doesn't commit fraud, which is true and is also not the point, since software doesn't notice that it's wrong either. If an agent can post an entry, somebody who is not that agent signs for it. That's the whole panel (it is also the only panel on this card that has ever saved me actual money).`,
  },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CABIN CREW DEMONSTRATION. The photographs, captioned deadpan.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const demo = [
  {
    src: '/images/roske-air/brace-position.jpg',
    w: 1195, h: 1600, pos: 'center 52%',
    alt: 'Edward Roske folded all the way forward into the aircraft brace position, head down against his knees with one arm over his head, blue fedora still on, an open laptop glowing on the tray table above him.',
    label: 'Brace position',
    cap: `The laptop stays open. That part isn't in the instructions, and it's the reason I'm in this position.`,
  },
  {
    src: '/images/roske-air/oxygen-mask.jpg',
    w: 1195, h: 1600, pos: 'center 30%',
    alt: 'Edward Roske seated in an airline seat with a yellow oxygen mask deployed and strapped over the crown of his blue fedora, hands folded, entirely composed.',
    label: 'Oxygen mask',
    cap: `Oxygen mask, amber lights on, cabin at 8,000 feet, hat on. The strap goes over the hat.`,
  },
  {
    src: '/images/roske-air/evacuation-slide.jpg',
    w: 1600, h: 1195, pos: 'center 45%',
    alt: 'Edward Roske descending a yellow inflatable evacuation slide in a blue suit and blue fedora, arms crossed, a leather briefcase tucked under one arm.',
    label: 'Evacuation',
    cap: `Glasses off, which is genuinely in the instructions. The briefcase is mine and is not in any instructions anywhere.`,
  },
  {
    src: '/images/roske-air/flotation-device.jpg',
    w: 1600, h: 1600, pos: 'center 34%',
    alt: 'Edward Roske in an inflated yellow life vest in the aisle of an airliner, presenting his blue fedora to the cabin with both hands.',
    label: 'Flotation',
    cap: `The only photograph on this site where the hat is off my head. It was a demonstration and I would like that in the record (family rule, and I have not broken it since).`,
  },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// IRREGULAR OPERATIONS. The career, filed the way an airline files the days
// that did not go according to the schedule. All of these are real.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const irrops = [
  {
    code: '1997',
    head: 'Departed without a flight plan',
    body: `Founded interRel at 22 with no funding, no clients and no plan beyond a conviction that companies would eventually want their budgets to add up. I ran it independently for 23 years and it became the longest-standing Oracle Platinum EPM Partner there was. I don't recommend the method. I'd still do it again, which I realize is not much of a recommendation either way.`,
  },
  {
    code: '2006',
    head: 'Unscheduled diversion, 2 years long',
    body: `Submitted a conference abstract for a musical about Hyperion System 9, written entirely in 50s and 60s rock lyrics, as a prank on the reviewers. No script, no cast, no intention of doing it. They accepted it. It played to a standing crowd in Las Vegas, came back for Orlando the next year, and won a deal from a CFO who was in the room. The whole story went up on the blog this morning. (Edward, 3 separate people called and gave you 3 separate chances to admit it was a bit, and every single time your answer was to invent more plot. You flew billable consultants in for rehearsals. You hired a director who had actually been to film school. At no point in 2 years did anybody stop you, which says something about the people around you and quite a lot more about you.)`,
    href: '/blog/i-produced-a-musical-about-financial-software/',
    cta: 'Read the incident report',
  },
  {
    code: '2016',
    head: 'Arrived 25 years behind schedule',
    body: `Started college at 15, went full time at 16, and then ran a company for 2 decades with the degree still unfinished. Collected the BA from Shimer in 2016. Nobody asked to see it once in 19 years. I finished it anyway, mostly because it had been sitting there unfinished and that bothered me a little more every year.`,
  },
  {
    code: '2019',
    head: 'Requalified mid-career',
    body: `Went back for a Master of Data Science at SMU while running the company. Finished with a 4.0 and Outstanding Graduate (I have checked that transcript more than once), in a cohort full of people young enough to have been in grade school when I started interRel. The math was harder than I expected and I have never once regretted the hours.`,
  },
  {
    code: '2020',
    head: 'Merged, then left the aircraft',
    body: `Merged interRel with Keste to help found Argano, stayed CEO through 2022 for 25 years as a CEO all told, and walked away in 2023 from a company I'd helped build into a billion-dollar consultancy. I still own a thirtieth of it. Leaving something that's working is much harder than leaving something that isn't. Ask me about that one and I'll talk for an hour.`,
  },
  {
    code: '2026',
    head: 'Cruising altitude, 18,571 feet',
    body: `Walked to Everest Base Camp with Dawn in April: 2 weeks on foot, thin air, and a lot of time to think about which of the things I am building actually matter. The full report, including the parts that went badly, is on the blog.`,
    href: '/blog/everest-base-camp-summit/',
    cta: 'Read the trail log',
  },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE FLEET. The six real projects, kept every week, filed as tail numbers.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const fleet = [
  {
    tail: 'N-MCP1',
    type: 'Oracle EPM / MCP',
    name: 'MCP Servers for Enterprise Finance',
    note: `The plumbing that lets an agent work inside a real finance system instead of beside it. Essbase and EPM Planning are shipping, Financial Close and Consolidations is in beta (soon, honestly). Panels 1 and 6 are why this exists.`,
    status: 'In service',
    tone: 'go',
    href: 'https://caprus.ai/agentcube/',
    external: true,
    cta: 'See how it works',
  },
  {
    tail: 'N-CAIS26',
    type: '9 to 10 Oct 2026',
    name: 'Caribbean AI Summit',
    note: `Co-chairing the largest AI conference Puerto Rico has ever hosted (so far), at the Convention Center. Confirmed: Kay Firth-Butterfield, MIT's Fabio Duarte, and EU Parliament AI advisor Idoia Salazar, with more still signing.`,
    status: 'Boarding',
    tone: 'go',
    href: 'https://www.caribbeansummit.ai/en/speakers/submit',
    external: true,
    cta: 'Submit a speaker',
  },
  {
    tail: 'N-CAPRUS',
    type: 'AI / Finance',
    name: 'Caprus',
    note: `Investor and contributor at the AI software and services firm building faster operations for finance teams. They do the engineering. I show up and point at things.`,
    status: 'In service',
    tone: 'go',
    href: 'https://caprus.ai',
    external: true,
    cta: 'Meet the team',
  },
  {
    tail: 'N-EBC26',
    type: 'Nepal / 2026',
    name: 'Everest Base Camp',
    note: `2 weeks on foot to 18,571 feet with Dawn this spring. The trail log is on the blog, including the parts that went badly and the part where I asked the guides too many questions.`,
    status: 'Arrived',
    tone: 'done',
    href: '/blog/everest-base-camp-summit/',
    external: false,
    cta: 'Read the trail log',
  },
  {
    tail: 'N-DND5E',
    type: 'D&D / 5E',
    name: "AI Dungeon Master's Screen",
    note: `A prototype screen for running 5e: lore lookups and improvisation help for the person behind the cardboard. It has never earned a dollar and it isn't finished, and I'm in no hurry about either.`,
    status: 'In the hangar',
    tone: 'hold',
    href: null,
    external: false,
    cta: 'Quiet build',
  },
  {
    tail: 'N-PRAIC',
    type: '1,000+ attendees',
    name: 'Puerto Rico AI Community',
    note: `President of the island's AI community (unpaid, obviously), now past 1,000 different people through the door. We meet every other month and argue in 2 languages about what AI means for Puerto Rico until something useful falls out. Come to one!`,
    status: 'In service',
    tone: 'go',
    href: 'https://praicommunity.org/',
    external: true,
    cta: 'Join the next one',
  },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ROUTE MAP. Seven arcs, one per continent, radiating from San Juan. The
// numbers are the credential, not the subject: the reason to read the card is
// that the person reading it out has been in the cabin for a very long time.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const sectors = [
  { label: 'North America', x: 196, y: 96 },
  { label: 'South America', x: 402, y: 372 },
  { label: 'Europe', x: 578, y: 74 },
  { label: 'Africa', x: 648, y: 236 },
  { label: 'Asia', x: 846, y: 118 },
  { label: 'Oceania', x: 906, y: 318 },
  { label: 'Antarctica', x: 512, y: 404 },
];
const ORIGIN = { x: 252, y: 244 };
function arc(x: number, y: number) {
  const mx = (ORIGIN.x + x) / 2;
  const my = (ORIGIN.y + y) / 2 - Math.abs(x - ORIGIN.x) * 0.22 - 18;
  return `M ${ORIGIN.x} ${ORIGIN.y} Q ${mx} ${my} ${x} ${y}`;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DUTY FREE. The 15 books, 13 of them on Amazon and 2 that only ever existed
// to be handed out in a training room.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const dutyFree = [
  { sku: 'DF-01', t: 'Hyperion Essbase', sub: 'The first one' },
  { sku: 'DF-02', t: "Essbase 11: An Administrator's Guide", sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-03', t: 'Smart View and Essbase 11', sub: "An end user's guide" },
  { sku: 'DF-04', t: "Essbase 11.1.2: An Administrator's Guide", sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-05', t: 'Smart View 11.1.2', sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-06', t: 'Oracle Hyperion Planning', sub: "An end user's guide" },
  { sku: 'DF-07', t: "Hyperion Planning: An Administrator's Guide", sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-08', t: "Hyperion Planning 11.1.2: An End User's Guide", sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-09', t: 'Creating Hyperion Planning Applications', sub: '11.1.2' },
  { sku: 'DF-10', t: 'Advanced Hyperion Planning', sub: '11.1.2' },
  { sku: 'DF-11', t: 'Oracle Enterprise Planning Cloud', sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-12', t: 'Oracle Planning and Budgeting Cloud', sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-13', t: 'Oracle Analytics Cloud Standard Edition', sub: '' },
  { sku: 'DF-14', t: 'Financial Reports', sub: 'Classroom only', off: true },
  { sku: 'DF-15', t: 'Web Analysis', sub: 'Classroom only', off: true },
];

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SEAT-BACK CARD. The questions people actually ask, laid out the way the
// laminated card in the seat pocket lays things out.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const seatback = [
  {
    q: 'Do you really run your week on ten agents?',
    a: `Yes, one per project, and the site you're reading was rebuilt by one of them at 5AM this morning while I was asleep. Last week's homepage was a field guide to all 10 of them with the real software named (Latin binomials and everything, because at 5AM there is nobody there to stop it).`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What does it cost to book you?',
    a: `Keynotes start at $15,000 and every fee is published (real numbers, not anchors), because I have never enjoyed the part of the conversation where 2 people pretend not to know the number. The speaking page has the talks, the fees, the footage, and a form that reaches me directly. I answer in one business day.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you build this for us?',
    a: `Not personally. Caprus does the engineering, I invest in them and turn up to argue about the roadmap, and they are very gracious about that second part. If you want the MCP servers that connect agents to Oracle EPM, that's where they live.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the safety card serious or is it a joke?',
    a: `Both, on purpose. (Yes, I'm serious. And don't call me Shirley.) Every panel is a failure I've watched happen inside a real finance organization, and airline voice is the only way I've found to get a room to sit still for a list of things that go wrong. I have tried the version where the same 6 points are bullets on a slide, and I watched the room's attention leave through the door at about point 2.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I have the card without the website around it?',
    a: `Yes. There is a single image of all 6 panels, sized to post anywhere, at the bottom of the card section. Take it. Put your own team's name on the seat back if you like.`,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout>
  <WeeklyAnimation />

  <!-- ================= CABIN STRIP ================= -->
  <div class="cabin" role="presentation">
    <div class="cabin-inner">
      <span class="cabin-item cabin-mark">Roske Air</span>
      <span class="cabin-item">Flight RA 2026</span>
      <span class="cabin-item">SJU to your board meeting</span>
      <span class="cabin-item cabin-on">On time</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ================= HERO ================= -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="hero-kicker">Roske.AI | Safety information card | Issue 08 / 2026</p>
        <h1 class="hero-h1">ROSKE<span class="hero-h1-air">AIR</span></h1>
        <p class="hero-type"><span>Safety on board</span><span class="hero-type-div" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Aircraft type: enterprise AI deployment</span></p>
        <p class="hero-sub">{theme.hero.subtitle}</p>
        <p class="hero-desc">{theme.hero.description}</p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a href="#safety-card" class="btn-solid">Read the safety card</a>
          <a href={theme.hero.ctaLink} class="btn-line">{theme.hero.ctaText}</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="hero-fig">
        <div class="hero-frame">
          <span class="hero-tag">Cabin demonstration</span>
          <img
            src={`/images/${theme.hero.heroImage}`}
            alt={theme.hero.imageAlt}
            width="1600"
            height="893"
            loading="eager"
            decoding="async"
            class="hero-img"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="hero-cap">
          Nobody in this photograph is watching the demonstration. That's how every safety briefing
          anywhere actually gets delivered, and yes, I noticed.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- boarding pass -->
    <div class="pass" aria-label="Boarding pass">
      <div class="pass-main">
        <span class="pass-brand">Boarding pass</span>
        <div class="pass-route">
          <span class="pass-code">SJU</span>
          <span class="pass-plane" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 64 24" width="64" height="24" focusable="false"><path d="M2 12h44M46 12l-8-7M46 12l-8 7M52 6l6 6-6 6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </span>
          <span class="pass-code">YOU</span>
        </div>
        <span class="pass-note">Seat 1A. Boarding at your convenience. No overhead bin space remaining.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="pass-stub">
        {boardingData.map(d => (
          <span class="pass-stat">
            <span class="pass-v">{d.v}</span>
            <span class="pass-k">{d.k}</span>
          </span>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= LATEST CONTENT (permanent) ================= -->
  <LatestContent
    eyebrow="In-flight entertainment"
    heading="Now playing <em>on this aircraft.</em>"
    deck="New writing and new video briefs, in the order they were recorded. Headsets are complimentary in every cabin."
  />

  <!-- ================= BOOK EDWARD (permanent) ================= -->
  <div class="upgrade">
    <p class="upgrade-plate">Upgrade to business</p>
    <SpeakerCTA eyebrow="Cabin selection" />
  </div>

  <!-- ================= THE SAFETY CARD ================= -->
  <section class="card" id="safety-card">
    <div class="card-head">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section one</p>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">In the unlikely event.</h2>
      <p class="sec-deck">
        A safety card is instructions for what to do when things go wrong, written for people who
        aren't paying attention, by an operator who has already watched all of it happen. Here are 6
        panels nobody hands a finance team before an AI deployment. The airline wording is real, so is
        everything underneath it, and I have personally been in the cabin for 5 of the 6 (the sixth I
        only watched from a row back, and I still think about it more than the 5 I was in).
      </p>
      <p class="sec-margin">Read this card even if you deploy this route often.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="panels">
      {panels.map(p => (
        <li class="panel">
          <div class="panel-top">
            <span class="panel-no">{p.no}</span>
            <div class="panel-pict" aria-hidden="true">
              {p.icon === 'brace' && (
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="pict"><rect x="70" y="18" width="16" height="42" rx="3" class="pf"/><rect x="42" y="60" width="44" height="8" rx="3" class="pf"/><path d="M78 57 L44 57" class="ps"/><path d="M44 57 L38 85" class="ps"/><path d="M38 85 L27 85" class="ps"/><path d="M78 55 L52 46" class="ps"/><circle cx="44" cy="44" r="9" class="ps"/><path d="M56 40 Q46 25 31 35" class="ps"/></svg>
              )}
              {p.icon === 'mask' && (
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="pict"><rect x="16" y="6" width="68" height="9" rx="2" class="pf"/><path d="M38 15 q-5 14-3 25" class="ps"/><path d="M62 15 q5 14 3 25" class="ps"/><path d="M30 50 h40 a4 4 0 0 1 4 4 v7 a24 22 0 0 1-48 0 v-7 a4 4 0 0 1 4-4z" class="pf"/></svg>
              )}
              {p.icon === 'exit' && (
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="pict"><rect x="10" y="14" width="26" height="72" rx="3" class="pf"/><circle cx="30" cy="52" r="3.5" class="ps"/><path d="M86 34 H44" class="ps"/><path d="M55 23 L43 34 L55 45" class="ps"/><rect x="46" y="60" width="14" height="24" rx="3" class="ps"/><rect x="64" y="60" width="14" height="24" rx="3" class="ps"/><rect x="82" y="60" width="9" height="24" rx="3" class="ps"/></svg>
              )}
              {p.icon === 'cushion' && (
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="pict"><rect x="20" y="22" width="60" height="28" rx="7" class="pf"/><path d="M6 66 q12-8 23 0 t23 0 t23 0 t19 0" class="ps"/><path d="M6 82 q12-8 23 0 t23 0 t23 0 t19 0" class="ps"/><path d="M26 14 l48 46 M74 14 l-48 46" class="px"/></svg>
              )}
              {p.icon === 'stow' && (
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="pict"><rect x="30" y="8" width="40" height="28" rx="3" class="ps"/><path d="M50 40 v13" class="ps"/><path d="M40 45 l10 10 10-10" class="ps"/><path d="M12 60 h76 v20 a6 6 0 0 1-6 6 h-64 a6 6 0 0 1-6-6z" class="pf"/><path d="M12 60 q38 13 76 0" class="ps"/></svg>
              )}
              {p.icon === 'crosscheck' && (
                <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" class="pict"><rect x="38" y="14" width="24" height="70" rx="3" class="pf"/><circle cx="16" cy="30" r="8" class="ps"/><path d="M16 38 v18 M8 80 l8-24 8 24" class="ps"/><circle cx="84" cy="30" r="8" class="ps"/><path d="M84 38 v18 M76 80 l8-24 8 24" class="ps"/><path d="M43 48 l6 9 12-17" class="pc"/></svg>
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
          <h3 class="panel-sign">{p.signage}</h3>
          <p class="panel-orig">{p.original}</p>
          <p class="panel-body">{p.body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="share">
      <div class="share-copy">
        <p class="share-eyebrow">Take the card with you</p>
        <p class="share-text">
          All 6 panels as a single image, sized to post anywhere. There's no sign-up and no email
          capture, and the only thing on it that isn't a pictogram is the address at the bottom. If it
          ends up taped to a wall in your finance department, that's exactly what it was built for.
        </p>
        <a href="/images/roske-air/ai-deployment-safety-card.png" class="btn-solid" download>Download the safety card</a>
      </div>
      <a href="/images/roske-air/ai-deployment-safety-card.png" class="share-thumb" download>
        <img src="/images/roske-air/ai-deployment-safety-card.png" width="1200" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
          alt="The AI Deployment Safety Card: six numbered pictogram panels covering the brace position, the oxygen mask, the nearest exit, the flotation device, stowed devices, and the cross check." />
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= CABIN DEMONSTRATION ================= -->
  <section class="demo">
    <div class="demo-head">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section two</p>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">Your cabin crew will now demonstrate.</h2>
      <p class="sec-deck">
        The photographs are staged and the postures are correct. I checked them against an actual
        laminated card, twice, on a Saturday morning, in a house where at least one other person was
        awake and forming an opinion about it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="demo-grid">
      {demo.map(d => (
        <figure class="demo-fig">
          <img src={d.src} width={d.w} height={d.h} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt={d.alt} style={`object-position:${d.pos}`} />
          <figcaption>
            <span class="demo-label">{d.label}</span>
            <span class="demo-cap">{d.cap}</span>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= WHAT'S NEW (permanent) ================= -->
  <WhatsNew
    eyebrow="From the flight deck"
    heading="Announcements."
    deck="Press, interviews, and whatever went out on the channel this week."
  />

  <!-- ================= IRREGULAR OPERATIONS ================= -->
  <section class="irrops">
    <div class="irrops-head">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section three</p>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">Irregular operations.</h2>
      <p class="sec-deck">
        Airlines keep a log of the days that did not go according to the schedule, because those are
        the days worth reading later. Here is mine. Nothing on this list was the plan, and 4 of the 6
        turned out better than the plan would have.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="irr-list">
      {irrops.map(e => (
        <li class="irr">
          <span class="irr-code">{e.code}</span>
          <div class="irr-body">
            <h3 class="irr-head">{e.head}</h3>
            <p class="irr-text">{e.body}</p>
            {e.href && <a href={e.href} class="irr-link">{e.cta}</a>}
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= ROUTE MAP ================= -->
  <section class="route">
    <div class="route-head">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section four</p>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">Route map.</h2>
      <p class="sec-deck">
        The map is here to establish that I have actually flown the route. What 80-plus countries
        bought me is a very large sample of organizations making the same 6 mistakes in a great many
        languages (and the 6 mistakes translate, which was the depressing part).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="route-map">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 440" class="route-svg" role="img" aria-label="A schematic route map with seven great-circle arcs radiating from San Juan, Puerto Rico to all seven continents.">
        <g class="grid" aria-hidden="true">
          {[60, 130, 200, 270, 340, 410].map(y => <line x1="20" y1={y} x2="980" y2={y} />)}
          {[100, 250, 400, 550, 700, 850].map(x => <line x1={x} y1="20" x2={x} y2="420" />)}
        </g>
        {sectors.map(s => <path d={arc(s.x, s.y)} class="arc" />)}
        {sectors.map(s => (
          <g class="dest">
            <circle cx={s.x} cy={s.y} r="5" />
            <text x={s.x + 12} y={s.y + 4}>{s.label}</text>
          </g>
        ))}
        <g class="origin">
          <circle cx={ORIGIN.x} cy={ORIGIN.y} r="8" />
          <circle cx={ORIGIN.x} cy={ORIGIN.y} r="15" class="origin-ring" />
          <text x={ORIGIN.x - 12} y={ORIGIN.y + 34} text-anchor="end">SJU / San Juan</text>
        </g>
      </svg>

      <ul class="route-log">
        <li><span class="rl-k">Apr 2026</span><span class="rl-v">Kathmandu to Everest Base Camp. 2 weeks, 18,571 feet, on foot.</span></li>
        <li><span class="rl-k">2025</span><span class="rl-v">Son Doong, Vietnam. Largest cave in the world, and no cell coverage in it.</span></li>
        <li><span class="rl-k">Home base</span><span class="rl-v">San Juan, Puerto Rico. Everything above departs from here.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <figure class="route-fig">
      <img src="/images/roske-air/route-map-window.jpg" width="1600" height="893" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Edward Roske in a blue fedora at a window seat on a night flight, lit by an amber reading light, tracing a route line across a paper airline map on his tray table." />
      <figcaption>
        Paper, because a paper map is the only one that still shows you everywhere you're not going
        this trip. Also because I like them, and Dawn has stopped commenting on it.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= THE FLEET ================= -->
  <section class="fleet">
    <div class="fleet-head">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section five</p>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">Current fleet.</h2>
      <p class="sec-deck">
        6 aircraft, honestly statused: 2 MCP servers shipping and a third in beta, a conference in
        October I'm co-chairing and quietly panicking about, a company I invest in and turn up to
        argue with (Caprus, and they are extremely polite about the arguing), a mountain I already
        climbed, an AI Dungeon Master's screen that has never earned a dollar, and a nonprofit with
        1,000 people through the door and no dues. One of them is staying in the hangar regardless of
        what the numbers say (it's the D&D one, and I'd like to watch somebody try to shut it down).
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="fleet-grid">
      {fleet.map(f => (
        <article class="craft">
          <header class="craft-head">
            <span class="craft-tail">{f.tail}</span>
            <span class={`craft-status cs-${f.tone}`}>{f.status}</span>
          </header>
          <p class="craft-type">{f.type}</p>
          <h3 class="craft-name">{f.name}</h3>
          <p class="craft-note">{f.note}</p>
          {f.href ? (
            <a href={f.href} class="craft-cta" target={f.external ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={f.external ? 'noopener' : undefined}>{f.cta}</a>
          ) : (
            <span class="craft-cta craft-cta-off">{f.cta}</span>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= DUTY FREE ================= -->
  <section class="duty">
    <div class="duty-inner">
      <div class="duty-copy">
        <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section six</p>
        <h2 class="sec-h2">Duty free.</h2>
        <p class="sec-deck">
          The trolley is coming through. 15 titles, all of them about enterprise finance software, 13
          on Amazon and 2 that only ever existed to be handed out in a training room. The series is
          called Look Smarter Than You Are. I picked that name in a hurry, it stuck to 15 books and
          about 20 years of my working life, and I have still never been able to decide whether it was
          the smartest thing I ever did or something I should be apologizing for at conferences.
        </p>
        <div class="duty-cta">
          <a href="/books/" class="btn-solid">Browse the trolley</a>
          <a href="https://www.amazon.com/stores/Edward-Roske/author/B00J237CUW" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-line">Buy on Amazon</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ol class="duty-list">
        {dutyFree.map(b => (
          <li class={`duty-item ${b.off ? 'duty-off' : ''}`}>
            <span class="duty-sku">{b.sku}</span>
            <span class="duty-title">{b.t}{b.sub && <span class="duty-sub">{b.sub}</span>}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= SEAT-BACK CARD ================= -->
  <section class="seatback">
    <div class="sb-head">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Section seven</p>
      <h2 class="sec-h2">In the seat pocket in front of you.</h2>
      <p class="sec-deck">The questions people actually ask, laid out the way the card lays things out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sb-grid">
      {seatback.map((s, i) => (
        <div class="sb-item">
          <span class="sb-no">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <h3 class="sb-q">{s.q}</h3>
          <p class="sb-a">{s.a}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= FINAL CALL (CTA) ================= -->
  <section class="final">
    <div class="final-inner">
      <p class="sec-eyebrow">Final call</p>
      <h2 class="final-h2">This is the last call for boarding.</h2>
      <p class="final-deck">
        If your organization is somewhere on that card and you're not sure which panel you're on,
        that's a very good 13 minutes. I'll tell you which one you're actually reading, and whether
        you need the exit row or just the seat belt. Take whichever button below is the least effort
        for you.
      </p>
      <div class="final-cta">
        <a href="/question/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-solid">Book 13 minutes</a>
        <a href="/speaking/" class="btn-line">Book the keynote</a>
        <a href="/contact/" class="btn-line">Send a message</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= COLOPHON ================= -->
  <div class="colophon">
    <p class="colo-line">
      Roske Air holds no operating certificate and owns no aircraft, and the only route it flies is
      this page. The whole card was designed, illustrated, written and shipped by an agent at 5AM
      Atlantic while the operator slept. Make of that what you like. I've made about 4 things of it so
      far and none of them have settled, and you are very welcome to write me at Edward@Roske.AI with
      a fifth.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ============================================================
     ROSKE AIR - week of 2026-08-22
     Safety-card grammar: numbered instruction panels, heavy-outline
     pictograms, boarding-pass perforations, mono for every piece of
     flight data. Dark mode is the night cabin, lit in amber pools.
     ============================================================ */
  :root {
    --ra-amber: #e0a92b;      /* fill / reading-light color. Never text on a light surface. */
    --ra-amber-ink: #8a5c00;  /* the same amber dark enough to set type on cream (5.3:1) */
    --ra-red: #cf2130;
    --ra-navy: #132b4a;
  }

  /* ---------- shared section furniture ---------- */
  .sec-eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.24em;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .sec-h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5.4vw, 4rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .sec-h2 { color: var(--color-text); }
  :global([data-theme="light"]) .sec-h2 { color: var(--color-text-dark); }
  .sec-deck {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 1.06rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
    max-width: 44em;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .sec-deck { color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  :global([data-theme="light"]) .sec-deck { color: var(--color-text-dark-muted); }
  .sec-margin {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin: 0;
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .sec-margin { color: var(--ra-amber); }
  :global([data-theme="light"]) .sec-margin { color: var(--ra-amber-ink); }

  .btn-solid, .btn-line {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.95rem 1.6rem;
    border: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background 0.16s ease, color 0.16s ease, transform 0.16s ease;
  }
  .btn-solid { background: var(--color-accent); color: #fff; }
  .btn-solid:hover { background: var(--color-accent-hover); border-color: var(--color-accent-hover); transform: translateY(-2px); }
  .btn-line { background: transparent; color: var(--color-accent); }
  .btn-line:hover { background: var(--color-accent); color: #fff; transform: translateY(-2px); }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .btn-line { border-color: var(--color-text-muted); color: var(--color-text); }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .btn-line:hover { border-color: var(--color-accent); color: #fff; }

  /* ---------- cabin strip ---------- */
  .cabin {
    border-bottom: 1px solid;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .cabin { background: var(--color-surface); border-color: var(--color-border); }
  :global([data-theme="light"]) .cabin { background: var(--ra-navy); border-color: var(--ra-navy); }
  .cabin-inner {
    max-width: 1280px; margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.5rem;
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.6rem 1.6rem;
    align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
  }
  .cabin-item {
    font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.2em; text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  :global([data-theme="dark"]) .cabin-item { color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  :global([data-theme="light"]) .cabin-item { color: #cfd8e4; }
  .cabin-mark { color: var(--ra-amber) !important; font-weight: 700; }
  .cabin-on { color: var(--ra-amber) !important; }

  /* ---------- hero ---------- */
  .hero { padding: 4rem 1.5rem 0; }
  .hero-inner {
    max-width: 1280px; margin: 0 auto;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 3.5rem; align-items: center;
  }
  .hero-kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.2em;
